              "text": "Calcium is a vital mineral that your body needs to build and maintain strong bones and teeth, ensure proper muscle function, assist with nerve signaling, and support heart health. \n\nThe Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) for most adults is **1,000 mg per day**, which increases to **1,200 mg per day** for women over 50 and men over 70. \n\nTo help you meet this goal, here is a comprehensive list of calcium-rich foods, broken down by category.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Dairy Sources\nDairy products are among the most well-known and highly bioavailable (easily absorbed) sources of calcium.\n*   **Plain Yogurt (Low-fat or Whole):** ~300 to 450 mg per cup (8 oz). (Yogurt is also a great source of probiotics).\n*   **Milk (Cow\u2019s Milk):** ~300 mg per cup (8 oz) (skim, low-fat, and whole milk have similar calcium contents).\n*   **Hard Cheeses (e.g., Parmesan, Cheddar, Swiss):** ~200 to 330 mg per ounce (28g). (Parmesan has the highest calcium content of almost any cheese).\n\n### 2. Seafood (with Bones)\nSmall, canned fish where you eat the soft, tiny bones are incredibly concentrated sources of calcium.\n*   **Canned Sardines (with bones, in oil):** ~325 mg per 3 oz (85g).\n*   **Canned Pink Salmon (with bones):** ~180 mg per 3 oz (85g).\n\n### 3. Plant-Based & Vegan Sources\nIf you are lactose intolerant, vegan, or simply prefer plant-based options, you can easily get enough calcium from these foods:\n*   **Tofu (Set with Calcium Sulfate):** ~860 mg per 1/2 cup. *(Check the ingredient label: tofu processed with calcium sulfate is one of the richest non-dairy sources of calcium).*\n*   **Dark Green Leafy Greens:**\n    *   **Collard Greens (cooked):** ~266 mg per cup.\n    *   **Kale (cooked):** ~180 mg per cup.\n    *   **Bok Choy (cooked):** ~160 mg per cup.\n    *   *Note on Spinach:* While spinach contains high amounts of calcium, it is also very high in oxalates (compounds that bind to calcium), meaning your body only absorbs a small fraction of it. \n*   **Seeds:** \n    *   **Chia Seeds:** ~177 mg per 2 tablespoons.\n    *   **Sesame Seeds / Tahini:** ~88 mg per tablespoon.\n*   **Nuts:**\n    *   **Almonds:** ~75 to 80 mg per ounce (about 23 almonds).\n*   **Beans and Lentils:**\n    *   **Edamame (Soybeans, cooked):** ~175 mg per cup.\n    *   **White Beans (cooked):** ~130 mg per cup.\n\n### 4. Fortified Foods & Drinks\nMany modern food products are artificially fortified with calcium to help people reach their daily requirements.\n*   **Fortified Plant Milks (Soy, Almond, Oat):** ~300 mg per cup (formulated to match the calcium profile of cow's milk).\n*   **Fortified Orange Juice:** ~300 to 350 mg per cup.\n*   **Fortified Breakfast Cereals:** ~250 to 1,000 mg per serving (depending on the brand).\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udca1 Two Crucial Tips for Calcium Absorption\n\n1.  **Don't forget Vitamin D:** Your body **cannot** absorb calcium properly without Vitamin D. Make sure you are getting enough sun exposure, or eat Vitamin D-rich foods like egg yolks, fatty fish, and fortified milks. \n2.  **Spread it out:** Your body absorbs calcium most efficiently in doses of **500 mg or less** at a time. Instead of trying to get all 1,000 mg in one meal, space your calcium-rich foods throughout the day.",
